course: medicine
lesson: 55
title: Food myths and fads
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Food myths spread because they sound simple: «carbs are bad», «never eat after 6», «bananas cure cramp», «superfoods give you powers»; science usually says the real answer is plainer and less exciting, a balanced plate.
explain: Tom gets cramp in the 80th minute and yells for a banana, but a banana takes far longer than ten minutes to digest, and scientists think most exercise cramp comes from tired muscles, not low potassium.
explain: «Superfood» is a word for selling, not a group in science: Aisha's aunt's £20 tub of goji, chia and spirulina costs as much as about 10 bags of apples and won't make anyone a superstar.
explain: Not every food tip is a fad: in the dreich, dark Scottish winter (dreich means grey and drizzly) Kirsty's mum gives her a vitamin D drop, because UK advice says everyone should think about it in autumn and winter, so the trick is to follow real advice, not fads either way.
fact: Bananas contain potassium, but most exercise cramp is thought to come from tired muscles, and a banana can't be digested fast enough to help mid-match; gentle stretching and rest help.
fact: Starchy carbs like bread, pasta, rice and potatoes are the body's main fuel for running about, and wholegrain ones are best.
fact: Children get enough protein from normal meals; protein shakes are made for adults and are not needed by children.
fact: «Superfood» is a marketing word, not a science category; no single food makes you faster or stronger.
fact: Extra vitamin pills don't make a healthy child faster, and too many vitamin gummies can be harmful, so keep them out of reach and follow the label or a doctor.
fact: UK advice is that everyone should think about taking 10 micrograms of vitamin D a day in autumn and winter, when there isn't enough sunshine for the skin to make it.
fact: Carb-loading is for long races of many hours, mostly for adults; before a short kids' race a normal meal is enough.
fact: A marathon is about 42 km, so a 2 km school cross-country is about 21 times shorter.
fact: The body doesn't turn food into fat because of the time on the clock, so a proper tea after evening training is right.
fact: The liver and kidneys clean the body all the time, so «detox» juice cleanses aren't needed and are a bad idea for growing children.
fact: Calling foods «cheat» or «clean» makes eating feel like a test; balanced meals most days and the odd treat without guilt is healthier.
fact: Online food posts are often adverts or opinions; a sensible check is to ask a parent, a school nurse or look at NHS advice.
fact: «Bussin'» is young American slang for really good, and «hen» is a Scottish way to call a girl or woman, like «love».
exercise: Do bananas cure cramp mid-match? | No, gentle stretching and rest help more | Yes, in about ten seconds | Yes, if you eat three | Only green bananas do
exercise: What do scientists think causes most exercise cramp? | Tired muscles | Too few bananas | Too much water | Cold boots
exercise: Are carbs bad for sporty kids? | No, they're the main fuel for running | Yes, they make you slow and heavy on the pitch | Yes, only adults are allowed to eat them | Only on match days, never during the week
exercise: Do children need protein shakes? | No, normal meals give enough | Yes, every day | Yes, before every match | Only if they want big muscles
exercise: What is a «superfood»? | A selling word, not a science group | A food that gives you special superpowers | A food only athletes may eat | A food approved by the Olympics
exercise: Do extra vitamin pills make a healthy child faster? | No, and too many can be harmful | Yes, the more pills, the faster you run | Yes, but only the chewy gummy ones | Only if they're taken just before a match
exercise: Which supplement does UK advice suggest in autumn and winter? | Vitamin D | Vitamin Z | Protein powder | Energy tablets
exercise: How much vitamin D a day is advised? | 10 micrograms | 10 grams | 10 tablets | 1,000 bottles
exercise: Is carb-loading needed before a 2 km school race? | No, a normal meal is enough | Yes, three plates of pasta the night before | Yes, for a whole week | Only if you want to win
exercise: Does food eaten after 6 pm turn into fat? | No, the clock doesn't change food | Yes, every single bite after 6 o'clock | Only bread and pasta eaten late at night | Only in winter, when the nights are long
exercise: Why are detox juice cleanses not needed? | The liver and kidneys clean the body | Juice is too thick for children to drink | Bodies never get dirty | Cleanses only work for adults
exercise: What's a healthier way to think about treats? | Balanced meals most days, the odd treat without guilt | A whole day of no rules every week | Never eat any treat ever | Treats only after winning
exercise: A food post online says something amazing. What's a sensible check? | Ask a parent or the school nurse, or look at NHS advice | Believe it if it has lots of likes | Try it on yourself straight away | Share it with all your friends
exercise: About how many times longer is a marathon than a 2 km race? | About 21 times | About 2 times | About 100 times | About the same
test: Tom, 80th minute, calf cramp: «get me a banana quick, I'm low on potassium!» What would actually help? | Gently stretching the calf and resting | Eating the banana, skin and all, as fast as he can | Sprinting hard to shake the cramp loose | Drinking a can of cola to get his sugar up
test: Emily's big sister says carbs are bad, so Emily skips bread and pasta and can't keep up at hockey. Why? | Starchy carbs are the main fuel for running | Hockey sticks are too heavy without pasta | She ate too many carbs | Carbs make legs heavy and slow in the second half
test: Logan: «all the gym bros drink protein shakes, they're bussin'». His mom: «you're nine, buddy». Who's right? | His mom; meals give a child enough protein | Logan; shakes make growing kids big and strong | Both; one shake a day is needed | Neither; kids need no protein
test: The aunt's tub costs £20 and a bag of apples £2. How many bags of apples for the same money? | 10 | 2 | 20 | 40
test: Nathan takes 3 gummy vitamins before every match: «pure turbo». What's true? | They don't make him faster and too many can harm | They make him a turbo player | Gummies are just sweets with no vitamins | He should take 10 for more speed
test: Kirsty: «but you said vitamins were a fad!» Mum: «not this one, hen». Why is vitamin D different? | UK advice backs it in the dark months | It's the tastiest vitamin | It cures every illness you catch in winter | It makes you taller instantly
test: Jayden eats 3 plates of pasta before a 2 km race, «carb loading innit», and gets a stitch and a bloated belly. Mistake? | Carb-loading is for very long races, not 2 km | He should have eaten 4 plates for more energy | He should have eaten them during the race | Pasta never has carbs in it
test: Lucy's friend: «never eat after 6 pm, it turns into fat». Lucy trains until 7. What should she do? | Eat her tea after training | Go to bed hungry on training nights | Eat only at 5:59, before the clock strikes | Skip training so she can eat by six
test: Owen sees «3-day juice-only detox for athletes». Should he do it for the season? | No, his liver and kidneys already clean his body | Yes, three days of juice will make him faster all season | Yes, but for a whole week | No, he should do a 10-day one instead
test: Kiara's coach: a «cheat day» every Sunday, no rules. Her mate: «sounds sus». Why might the mate have a point? | Labelling food «cheat» makes eating feel like a test | Sundays are bad for digestion | Treats are illegal on Sundays | Coaches are never right about food
test: Sam says if one banana is good for cramp, a whole bunch before every match must stop cramp forever. What's wrong with his thinking? | Cramp isn't mainly caused by low potassium, so more bananas won't stop it | Nothing, more bananas means no cramp | Bananas give you cramp | Only one banana a year is safe
test: A gym advert says «this powder made our star fast». What's the clever question to ask? | Did years of training, food and sleep make him fast? | Which flavour is the fastest? | How many tubs should I buy? | Is it cheaper in bulk?
exam: Identify the principal cause to which exercise-associated muscular cramp is at present attributed. | Muscular fatigue | A deficiency of bananas | An excess of water | The temperature of the footwear
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the designation «superfood». | It is a term of marketing without scientific standing | It denotes a scientifically defined category of food | It denotes a food conferring supernatural abilities upon the eater | It denotes food permitted solely to athletes
exam: Pursuant to guidance issued in the United Kingdom, determine the daily quantity of vitamin D which all persons should consider taking during autumn and winter. | Ten micrograms | Ten grams | Ten milligrams per hour | No quantity whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of so-called detox regimens? | They are unnecessary, the liver and kidneys performing that function | They are indispensable to the health of children | They remove all toxins within one hour | They replace the function of the kidneys entirely
